## Task 1 — Finding The Hidden User

### What I Did

```bash
cat /tmp/spy_case/logs/passwd.bak
```

**Breaking down this command completely:**

`cat` — short for "concatenate." This command reads a file and prints its entire contents to the terminal. It's one of the most used commands in Linux and CTFs. The name comes from its original purpose of concatenating (joining) multiple files together, but today it's mostly used just to read files.

`/tmp/spy_case/logs/passwd.bak` — this is an absolute path. In Linux, paths starting with `/` always start from the root of the filesystem. This means: go to root `/`, then into `tmp`, then `spy_case`, then `logs`, then open the file named `passwd.bak`.

### What I Found

```
root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash
daemon:x:1:1:daemon:/usr/sbin:/usr/sbin/nologin
www-data:x:33:33:www-data:/var/www:/usr/sbin/nologin
sp3y_4g3nt:x:1337:1337::/home/sp3y_4g3nt:/bin/bash
```

### Understanding The passwd File Format

The real `/etc/passwd` file on any Linux system stores user account information. Every line represents one user and follows this exact format:

```
username:password:UID:GID:comment:home_directory:shell
```

Breaking down the suspicious line:

```
sp3y_4g3nt : x  : 1337 : 1337 : : /home/sp3y_4g3nt : /bin/bash
     │       │     │      │    │         │                  │
     │       │     │      │    │         │                  └── Shell the user runs
     │       │     │      │    │         └── Home directory
     │       │     │      │    └── Comment (empty here)
     │       │     │      └── GID (Group ID)
     │       │     └── UID (User ID)
     │       └── Password stored in /etc/shadow (x means go look there)
     └── Username
```

### Why UID 1337 Is Suspicious

On any standard Linux system, UIDs follow this pattern:

```
UID 0          = root (most powerful user, can do anything)
UID 1-999      = system accounts (daemons, services like www-data)
UID 1000+      = normal human users (created automatically in order: 1000, 1001, 1002...)
```

When a new user is created normally by the system, Linux assigns the next available UID automatically. So a legitimate system would have users with UIDs like 1000, 1001, 1002 — in order.

UID 1337 was chosen manually and deliberately by the spy. No automatic system process would skip from 1002 to 1337. This is a red flag that someone intentionally created this account and specifically chose 1337.

In hacker culture, 1337 means "leet" which is short for "elite." It has been used by hackers since the 1980s as a kind of signature. Seeing UID 1337 in a passwd file during a real investigation is basically the attacker leaving their calling card.

**Real world application:** During penetration testing and incident response, one of the first things investigators check is `/etc/passwd` for unusual UIDs, usernames with strange characters (like underscores and numbers in `sp3y_4g3nt`), and accounts with `/bin/bash` shell access that shouldn't have it.

